  "worked_solution": "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $\u2205.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set $\u2205$ is ${\u2205}.$ Therefore, $P(P(\u2205))={\u2205,{\u2205}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set ${\u2205}.$ So, its power set must be ${\u2205,{\u2205}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({\u2205}))={\u2205,{\u2205},{{\u2205}},{\u2205,{\u2205}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set ${0}.$ So, its power set must be ${\u2205,{0}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({0}))={\u2205,{\u2205},{{0}},{\u2205,{0}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
